{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WCTE2wPEKByLEPf6zLUcCL","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"insights from negative results\" NLP workshop acceptance rate review criteria 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CFP | Insights 2024\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/2021/cfp/\"},{\"title\":\"Insights 2024 | Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"NAACL 2025 The 5th Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.insights-1.0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Third Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/third-workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp-0\"},{\"title\":\"INSIGHTS FROM NEGATIVE RESULTS IN NLP. WORKSHOP. 2ND 2022. (INSIGHTS 2022) - proceedings.com\",\"url\":\"https://www.proceedings.com/67716.html\"},{\"title\":\"Insights 2024 The 5th Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2024.insights-1.0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Proceedings of the Third Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://publications.hse.ru/en/books/674334187\"},{\"title\":\"[Corpora-List] Second Call For Papers: The Fourth Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (co-located with NAACL 2024)\",\"url\":\"https://www.mail-archive.com/corpora@list.elra.info/msg02706.html\"},{\"title\":\"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP - ACL Anthology\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/venues/insights/\"},{\"title\":\"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp-0\"}]\n\nBased on my web search results, here's information about the \"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\":\n\n## Acceptance Rates\n\nFor the 2025 workshop (5th iteration), there was a 64% acceptance rate with 16 papers accepted out of 23 submissions. The 2024 workshop accepted 19 short papers at a 57.5% acceptance rate. The 2022 workshop accepted 24 short papers at a 55.8% acceptance rate.\n\n## Review Criteria\n\nA successful negative results paper would contribute broadly applicable recommendations for training/fine-tuning/prompting, especially if X that didn't work is something many practitioners would think reasonable to try with explanation/hypothesis; ablation studies showing component contributions differ from initial reports; datasets or probing tasks showing previous approaches don't generalize; trivial baselines that work suspiciously well; cross-lingual studies showing technique success varies by language; experiments on result instability due to hardware/random initializations/preprocessing; or theoretical arguments for why X should not work.\n\n## General Submission Guidelines\n\nThe workshop cannot accept work that will be or has been published elsewhere and that will be submitted to other meetings whose review periods overlap with Insights. If a paper has been rejected from another venue, authors may provide original reviews and author response, though new reviewers won't access this information but organizers can consider that the paper has been revised and improved.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"4fb91aa1-0a64-4125-a2da-6ba18c5bb05c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:46:50.070Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"insights from negative results\" NLP workshop acceptance rate review criteria 2024 2025","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01U9FzBgk3Qs75G6hbhvKQmJ","content":[{"title":"CFP | Insights 2024","url":"https://insights-workshop.github.io/2021/cfp/"},{"title":"Insights 2024 | Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP","url":"https://insights-workshop.github.io/"},{"title":"NAACL 2025 The 5th Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.insights-1.0.pdf"},{"title":"The Third Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/third-workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp-0"},{"title":"INSIGHTS FROM NEGATIVE RESULTS IN NLP. WORKSHOP. 2ND 2022. (INSIGHTS 2022) - proceedings.com","url":"https://www.proceedings.com/67716.html"},{"title":"Insights 2024 The 5th Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.insights-1.0.pdf"},{"title":"Proceedings of the Third Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP","url":"https://publications.hse.ru/en/books/674334187"},{"title":"[Corpora-List] Second Call For Papers: The Fourth Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (co-located with NAACL 2024)","url":"https://www.mail-archive.com/corpora@list.elra.info/msg02706.html"},{"title":"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP - ACL Anthology","url":"https://aclanthology.org/venues/insights/"},{"title":"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp-0"}]},"Based on my web search results, here's information about the \"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\":\n\n## Acceptance Rates\n\nFor the 2025 workshop (5th iteration), there was a 64% acceptance rate with 16 papers accepted out of 23 submissions. The 2024 workshop accepted 19 short papers at a 57.5% acceptance rate. The 2022 workshop accepted 24 short papers at a 55.8% acceptance rate.\n\n## Review Criteria\n\nA successful negative results paper would contribute broadly applicable recommendations for training/fine-tuning/prompting, especially if X that didn't work is something many practitioners would think reasonable to try with explanation/hypothesis; ablation studies showing component contributions differ from initial reports; datasets or probing tasks showing previous approaches don't generalize; trivial baselines that work suspiciously well; cross-lingual studies showing technique success varies by language; experiments on result instability due to hardware/random initializations/preprocessing; or theoretical arguments for why X should not work.\n\n## General Submission Guidelines\n\nThe workshop cannot accept work that will be or has been published elsewhere and that will be submitted to other meetings whose review periods overlap with Insights. If a paper has been rejected from another venue, authors may provide original reviews and author response, though new reviewers won't access this information but organizers can consider that the paper has been revised and improved."],"durationSeconds":7.670189490999998}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GdF2bEFyAnTvVdiHDRCUVM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me synthesize:\n\n**Venue: Insights from Negative Results in NLP @ EMNLP 2026**\n- Workshop focused on negative results in NLP\n- Uses ARR review framework (soundness/excitement/overall)\n- Acceptance rate ~55-64% historically\n- Values: broadly applicable recommendations, ablation studies, result instability experiments, explanations for why things don't work\n\n**ARR Scoring Scale (since Feb 2025):**\n- Soundness: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n\n\n- Excitement: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- Overall: 1-5 scale (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next, 1=Do not resubmit)\n\nNow I'm setting up the full review dispatch with two reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work.\n\nI'll run them in parallel as independent agents, then guide them through the roundtable process: independent reviews first, then cross-sharing of perspectives, convergence on key points, and finally a synthesized verdict. Let me launch both reviewers now and handle the synthesis myself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":902,"cache_read_input_tokens":64380,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":902},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"ef3f8029-dcc6-41bc-9990-f6e28a72c71b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GdF2bEFyAnTvVdiHDRCUVM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've gathered the venue guidelines and read both the PDF and experiment context. Now I'll dispatch R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":902,"cache_read_input_tokens":64380,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":902},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"c3bc2339-af53-4343-9f16-4e82c52b48b0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GdF2bEFyAnTvVdiHDRCUVM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful in tool-use, e.g., PALADIN's +57pp on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under 3 conditions: Vanilla, TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled injections). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal (1) shared threshold-plateau at 10-25% injection, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Results partially replicate on WebShop. Scale ablation to 50K shows deficit persists.\n\n## Key Tables/Results from the paper:\n- **Table 1**: Core 3-seed results: Vanilla 65.3±3.3, Random 70.9±3.3, TaxEI 64.5±3.3 (but note: abstract says TaxEI=56.7% and SD=8.6pp, while Table 1 shows 64.5±3.3 — this is a discrepancy)\n- **Table 2**: Dose-response (seed=42): taxonomy penalty constant at ~6.4pp across 10/25/50% doses\n- **Table 3**: Taxonomy penalty across 3 seeds × 3 doses: all 6.4pp, SD≈0.4pp\n- **Table 4**: Loss decomposition: TaxEI clean loss inflation 16.6%, cosine similarity 0.28\n- **Table 5**: 24 qualitative failure cases, 62.5% \"wrong learning\"\n- **Table 6**: WebShop replication: variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp\n- **Table 7**: Cross-environment consistency\n- **Table 8**: Scale ablation 9K/25K/50K: deficit persists\n- **Table 9**: Few-shot recovery vs SFT: 3-shot=66.0% vs TaxEI SFT=56.7%\n- **Table 10**: Per-operator recovery: C4 +6.1pp (local), P2 -8.4pp (global)\n- **Table 11**: Statistical tests: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n- **Table 12**: Task-structural comparison\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. Random Dose-Response Curve Completion\n2. Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication  \n3. Training Dynamics Analysis\n4. Qualitative Failure Analysis (24 cases)\n5. Second Environment Validation (WebShop, 2-seed)\n6. Scale Ablation (25K/50K)\n7. Few-Shot Recovery Prompting\n8. Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive (Multi-Seed)\n9. Pooled Statistical Power Update\n\nThe paper appears to cover all 9 imagined experiments.\n\n## CRITICAL DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE\nThe abstract claims TaxEI mean=56.7%, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=8.6pp vs 3.3pp. But Table 1 shows TaxEI mean=64.5±3.3. These numbers don't match. The 56.7% and 8.6pp figures appear only in later tables (Table 8 shows 56.7 for 9K TaxEI, Table 1 shows 64.5 for core results). This could indicate the abstract is reporting a different metric/configuration than Table 1 without clear explanation, or it could be a data inconsistency.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodology soundness**: experimental design, controls, confounds\n2. **Statistical rigor**: sample sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons\n3. **Reproducibility**: enough detail to reproduce?\n4. **Data consistency**: are all tables/claims internally consistent?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: completeness of ablations and controls\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a negative results paper — the bar for \"proving the negative\" is high.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":902,"cache_read_input_tokens":64380,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":902},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"7ca446ad-0acf-41cb-be09-28772bf1532f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae8d79806effbcf65","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful in tool-use, e.g., PALADIN's +57pp on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under 3 conditions: Vanilla, TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled injections). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal (1) shared threshold-plateau at 10-25% injection, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Results partially replicate on WebShop. Scale ablation to 50K shows deficit persists.\n\n## Key Tables/Results from the paper:\n- **Table 1**: Core 3-seed results: Vanilla 65.3±3.3, Random 70.9±3.3, TaxEI 64.5±3.3 (but note: abstract says TaxEI=56.7% and SD=8.6pp, while Table 1 shows 64.5±3.3 — this is a discrepancy)\n- **Table 2**: Dose-response (seed=42): taxonomy penalty constant at ~6.4pp across 10/25/50% doses\n- **Table 3**: Taxonomy penalty across 3 seeds × 3 doses: all 6.4pp, SD≈0.4pp\n- **Table 4**: Loss decomposition: TaxEI clean loss inflation 16.6%, cosine similarity 0.28\n- **Table 5**: 24 qualitative failure cases, 62.5% \"wrong learning\"\n- **Table 6**: WebShop replication: variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp\n- **Table 7**: Cross-environment consistency\n- **Table 8**: Scale ablation 9K/25K/50K: deficit persists\n- **Table 9**: Few-shot recovery vs SFT: 3-shot=66.0% vs TaxEI SFT=56.7%\n- **Table 10**: Per-operator recovery: C4 +6.1pp (local), P2 -8.4pp (global)\n- **Table 11**: Statistical tests: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n- **Table 12**: Task-structural comparison\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. Random Dose-Response Curve Completion\n2. Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication  \n3. Training Dynamics Analysis\n4. Qualitative Failure Analysis (24 cases)\n5. Second Environment Validation (WebShop, 2-seed)\n6. Scale Ablation (25K/50K)\n7. Few-Shot Recovery Prompting\n8. Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive (Multi-Seed)\n9. Pooled Statistical Power Update\n\nThe paper appears to cover all 9 imagined experiments.\n\n## CRITICAL DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE\nThe abstract claims TaxEI mean=56.7%, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=8.6pp vs 3.3pp. But Table 1 shows TaxEI mean=64.5±3.3. These numbers don't match. The 56.7% and 8.6pp figures appear only in later tables (Table 8 shows 56.7 for 9K TaxEI, Table 1 shows 64.5 for core results). This could indicate the abstract is reporting a different metric/configuration than Table 1 without clear explanation, or it could be a data inconsistency.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodology soundness**: experimental design, controls, confounds\n2. **Statistical rigor**: sample sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons\n3. **Reproducibility**: enough detail to reproduce?\n4. **Data consistency**: are all tables/claims internally consistent?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: completeness of ablations and controls\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a negative results paper — the bar for \"proving the negative\" is high.","uuid":"f8cc9ab9-ad68-4f8b-b4af-8402e18af8d4","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful in tool-use, e.g., PALADIN's +57pp on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under 3 conditions: Vanilla, TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled injections). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal (1) shared threshold-plateau at 10-25% injection, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Results partially replicate on WebShop. Scale ablation to 50K shows deficit persists.\n\n## Key Tables/Results from the paper:\n- **Table 1**: Core 3-seed results: Vanilla 65.3±3.3, Random 70.9±3.3, TaxEI 64.5±3.3 (but note: abstract says TaxEI=56.7% and SD=8.6pp, while Table 1 shows 64.5±3.3 — this is a discrepancy)\n- **Table 2**: Dose-response (seed=42): taxonomy penalty constant at ~6.4pp across 10/25/50% doses\n- **Table 3**: Taxonomy penalty across 3 seeds × 3 doses: all 6.4pp, SD≈0.4pp\n- **Table 4**: Loss decomposition: TaxEI clean loss inflation 16.6%, cosine similarity 0.28\n- **Table 5**: 24 qualitative failure cases, 62.5% \"wrong learning\"\n- **Table 6**: WebShop replication: variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp\n- **Table 7**: Cross-environment consistency\n- **Table 8**: Scale ablation 9K/25K/50K: deficit persists\n- **Table 9**: Few-shot recovery vs SFT: 3-shot=66.0% vs TaxEI SFT=56.7%\n- **Table 10**: Per-operator recovery: C4 +6.1pp (local), P2 -8.4pp (global)\n- **Table 11**: Statistical tests: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n- **Table 12**: Task-structural comparison\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. Random Dose-Response Curve Completion\n2. Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication  \n3. Training Dynamics Analysis\n4. Qualitative Failure Analysis (24 cases)\n5. Second Environment Validation (WebShop, 2-seed)\n6. Scale Ablation (25K/50K)\n7. Few-Shot Recovery Prompting\n8. Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive (Multi-Seed)\n9. Pooled Statistical Power Update\n\nThe paper appears to cover all 9 imagined experiments.\n\n## CRITICAL DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE\nThe abstract claims TaxEI mean=56.7%, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=8.6pp vs 3.3pp. But Table 1 shows TaxEI mean=64.5±3.3. These numbers don't match. The 56.7% and 8.6pp figures appear only in later tables (Table 8 shows 56.7 for 9K TaxEI, Table 1 shows 64.5 for core results). This could indicate the abstract is reporting a different metric/configuration than Table 1 without clear explanation, or it could be a data inconsistency.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodology soundness**: experimental design, controls, confounds\n2. **Statistical rigor**: sample sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons\n3. **Reproducibility**: enough detail to reproduce?\n4. **Data consistency**: are all tables/claims internally consistent?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: completeness of ablations and controls\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a negative results paper — the bar for \"proving the negative\" is high."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"fa8a808c-5e41-432c-b149-e98c98cdceb9","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:47:36.318Z"}
