{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SLGrCKCtKeMAHcEkeTVTbB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Random Dose-Response Curve Completion\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Random injection 的 10%/25%/50% 剂量-响应曲线（固定 4,358 总样本，seed=42）， 与 TaxEI 剂量-响应并行对比，验证共享阈值-平台效应和恒定 taxonomy penalty。\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"random_inject10_pct\": 68.1,\n7\t      \"random_inject25_pct\": 55.3,\n8\t      \"random_inject50_pct\": 57.4,\n9\t      \"random_10to25_drop_pp\": 12.8,\n10\t      \"taxei_10to25_drop_pp\": 12.8,\n11\t      \"shared_drop_difference_pp\": 0.0,\n12\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_10_pp\": 6.4,\n13\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_25_pp\": 6.4,\n14\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_50_pp\": 6.3,\n15\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_mean_pp\": 6.37,\n16\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_sd_pp\": 0.06\n17\t    },\n18\t    \"tables\": [\n19\t      {\n20\t        \"title\": \"Combined Dose-Response Table (seed=42, fixed 4,358 total)\",\n21\t        \"content\": \"| Dose  | Vanilla | TaxEI  | Random | Δ(R−T) | TaxEI Δ(vs 10%) | Random Δ(vs 10%) |\\n|-------|---------|--------|--------|--------|------------------|------------------|\\n| 0%    | 61.7%   | 61.7%  | 61.7%  | 0.0    | —                | —                |\\n| 10%   | —       | 61.7%  | 68.1%  | +6.4   | 0.0              | 0.0              |\\n| 25%   | —       | 48.9%  | 55.3%  | +6.4   | −12.8            | −12.8            |\\n| 50%   | —       | 51.1%  | 57.4%  | +6.3   | −10.6            | −10.7            |\\n\"\n22\t      }\n23\t    ],\n24\t    \"figures\": [],\n25\t    \"analysis\": \"剂量-响应曲线揭示两个可分离的独立效应：(1) 共享的信噪比阈值——两种注入方法在同一临界区间 [10%, 25%] 发生等幅崩溃，证明这是 error injection 的 fundamental property 而非 taxonomy 特异性； (2) 恒定的 taxonomy penalty——TaxEI 在每个 dose 点均落后 Random 恰好 ~6.4pp，说明 taxonomy label 引入的是正交于注入剂量的 additive bias。Random 10% 的 +6.4pp 增益是意外发现，提供了重要的正面 对照：说明少量未标记噪声注入本身可以是有效的 data augmentation，但 taxonomy label 消除了这一好处。\\n\\n\\nRandom 10% = 68.1% 高于 vanilla 6.4pp，反映纯数据增强效应——无标签的噪声注入在低剂量下增加 训练分布多样性，实质上是合法的 data augmentation。TaxEI 10% = 61.7% 无此增益，说明 taxonomy label 抵消了增强效应，将 +6.4pp augmentation bonus 精确归零。10→25% 两种方法同步崩溃 12.8pp （Random 68.1→55.3, TaxEI 61.7→48.9），证明阈值效应是 error injection 的通用属性：当注入比例 超过 ~15-20%，clean 梯度信号被稀释至临界点以下，模型无法维持正确的 action distribution。 50% 处两者均回升 ~2pp（平台效应），因为极高注入比例下 clean 和 injected 样本的梯度已充分混合， 进一步增加注入不再额外恶化。6.4/6.4/6.3pp 的恒定 taxonomy penalty 在三个独立 dose 点保持 一致（SD=0.06），精确对应 3 个 ALFWorld 任务的分辨率（3/47=6.38pp），强烈支持 taxonomy label 引入的是与剂量无关的 systematic bias——结构化错误模式教会模型特定的错误行为，而非仅增加噪声。\\n\"\n26\t  },\n27\t  {\n28\t    \"name\": \"Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication\",\n29\t    \"description\": \"在 seed=0 和 seed=123 上重复完整的剂量-响应实验（10%/25%/50%，固定 4,358 总样本）， 验证阈值-平台效应和 taxonomy penalty 的跨 seed 稳定性。共 12 次训练 + 12 次 eval。\\n\",\n30\t    \"results\": {\n31\t      \"seed0_taxei_inject10_pct\": 68.1,\n32\t      \"seed0_taxei_inject25_pct\": 55.3,\n33\t      \"seed0_taxei_inject50_pct\": 57.4,\n34\t      \"seed0_random_inject10_pct\": 74.5,\n35\t      \"seed0_random_inject25_pct\": 61.7,\n36\t      \"seed0_random_inject50_pct\": 63.8,\n37\t      \"seed0_shared_10to25_drop_pp\": 12.8,\n38\t      \"seed0_taxonomy_penalty_mean_pp\": 6.4,\n39\t      \"seed123_taxei_inject10_pct\": 63.8,\n40\t      \"seed123_taxei_inject25_pct\": 53.2,\n41\t      \"seed123_taxei_inject50_pct\": 53.2,\n42\t      \"seed123_random_inject10_pct\": 70.2,\n43\t      \"seed123_random_inject25_pct\": 59.6,\n44\t      \"seed123_random_inject50_pct\": 59.6,\n45\t      \"seed123_shared_10to25_drop_pp\": 10.6,\n46\t      \"seed123_taxonomy_penalty_mean_pp\": 6.4,\n47\t      \"all_seeds_10to25_drop_mean_pp\": 12.1,\n48\t      \"all_seeds_10to25_drop_sd_pp\": 1.1,\n49\t      \"all_seeds_taxonomy_penalty_mean_pp\": 6.37,\n50\t      \"all_seeds_taxonomy_penalty_sd_pp\": 0.4,\n51\t      \"dose_response_cross_seed_agreement\": 1.0\n52\t    },\n53\t    \"tables\": [\n54\t      {\n55\t        \"title\": \"Multi-Seed Dose-Response: TaxEI (fixed 4,358 total)\",\n56\t        \"content\": \"| Dose | Seed 42 | Seed 0 | Seed 123 | Mean   | SD    |\\n|------|---------|--------|----------|--------|-------|\\n| 0%   | 61.7%   | 68.1%  | 66.0%    | 65.3%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 10%  | 61.7%   | 68.1%  | 63.8%    | 64.5%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 25%  | 48.9%   | 55.3%  | 53.2%    | 52.5%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 50%  | 51.1%   | 57.4%  | 53.2%    | 53.9%  | 3.2pp |\\n\"\n57\t      },\n58\t      {\n59\t        \"title\": \"Multi-Seed Dose-Response: Random (fixed 4,358 total)\",\n60\t        \"content\": \"| Dose | Seed 42 | Seed 0 | Seed 123 | Mean   | SD    |\\n|------|---------|--------|----------|--------|-------|\\n| 0%   | 61.7%   | 68.1%  | 66.0%    | 65.3%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 10%  | 68.1%   | 74.5%  | 70.2%    | 70.9%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 25%  | 55.3%   | 61.7%  | 59.6%    | 58.9%  | 3.3pp |\\n| 50%  | 57.4%   | 63.8%  | 59.6%    | 60.3%  | 3.3pp |\\n\"\n61\t      },\n62\t      {\n63\t        \"title\": \"Taxonomy Penalty Across Seeds × Doses\",\n64\t        \"content\": \"| Dose | Seed 42 | Seed 0 | Seed 123 | Mean  |\\n|------|---------|--------|----------|-------|\\n| 10%  | 6.4pp   | 6.4pp  | 6.4pp    | 6.4pp |\\n| 25%  | 6.4pp   | 6.4pp  | 6.4pp    | 6.4pp |\\n| 50%  | 6.3pp   | 6.4pp  | 6.4pp    | 6.4pp |\\n\"\n65\t      },\n66\t      {\n67\t        \"title\": \"Shared 10→25% Drop Across Seeds\",\n68\t        \"content\": \"| Seed | TaxEI Drop | Random Drop | Difference |\\n|------|------------|-------------|------------|\\n| 42   | 12.8pp     | 12.8pp      | 0.0pp      |\\n| 0    | 12.8pp     | 12.8pp      | 0.0pp      |\\n| 123  | 10.6pp     | 10.6pp      | 0.0pp      |\\n| Mean | 12.1pp     | 12.1pp      | 0.0pp      |\\n\"\n69\t      }\n70\t    ],\n71\t    \"figures\": [],\n72\t    \"analysis\": \"多 seed 剂量-响应是 best 档的决定性实验。9 个独立的 taxonomy penalty 观测点（3 seed × 3 dose） 形成的 SD ≈ 0.4pp 远低于 1.5pp 阈值，为 systematic additive bias 假说提供了近乎完美的证据。 共享阈值-平台在所有 seed 复现（10→25% drop 均 ≥10pp），最大跨 seed 偏差 2.2pp（seed 123 的 10.6pp vs seed 42 的 12.8pp），落在 ≤3pp 预算内。值得注意的是，每个 seed 内 TaxEI 和 Random 的 drop 幅度精确匹配（差异 ≤0.1pp），再次证明阈值效应是数据组成而非方法特异性的结果。 这些结果使论文可以做出 definitive statement：error injection 的剂量-响应是 task-structural 属性，taxonomy label 只在此基础上叠加恒定惩罚。\\n\\n\\n阈值-平台效应的物理机制是数据组成决定的信噪比边界，与随机初始化无关：当 clean 样本占比从 90%（dose=10%）降至 75%（dose=25%）时，clean 梯度信号不再足以维持正确的 action distribution。 这一边界由数据比例而非 seed 决定，因此跨 seed 高度稳定。Seed 42 的 12.8pp 在 seed 0 处精确 复现（12.8pp），seed 123 略低（10.6pp），反映 seed 123 的 vanilla baseline 较低（66.0%）， 留给崩溃的绝对空间较小——但所有 seed 均在 [10.0, 15.0]pp 范围内。Taxonomy penalty 的跨 seed 稳定性（SD ≈ 0.4pp）更进一步确认这是一个由 label 结构决定的 systematic bias：taxonomy label 在每个 seed 的每个 dose 点均将 3 个额外任务从成功翻转为失败，对应于 47 任务中固定的一组 对 taxonomy-specific 错误模式敏感的任务。Seed 0 的 Random 10% = 74.5% 高于其他 seed， 但仍符合 data augmentation 效应——seed 0 的 vanilla baseline 已较高（68.1%）， augmentation bonus 叠加后接近 ALFWorld 的性能天花板。\\n\"\n73\t  },\n74\t  {\n75\t    \"name\": \"Training Dynamics Analysis\",\n76\t    \"description\": \"从 v4 seed=42 的 training log 中提取逐 step loss 曲线，分离 clean 和 injected 样本的 loss， 分析注入样本对 clean 学习信号的污染程度。不需要新训练。\\n\",\n77\t    \"results\": {\n78\t      \"vanilla_clean_loss_final\": 0.29,\n79\t      \"taxei_overall_loss_final\": 0.355,\n80\t      \"taxei_clean_loss_final\": 0.338,\n81\t      \"taxei_injected_loss_final\": 0.482,\n82\t      \"random_overall_loss_final\": 0.355,\n83\t      \"random_clean_loss_final\": 0.318,\n84\t      \"random_injected_loss_final\": 0.445,\n85\t      \"clean_loss_inflation_taxei_pct\": 16.6,\n86\t      \"clean_loss_inflation_random_pct\": 9.7,\n87\t      \"injected_to_clean_loss_ratio_taxei\": 1.43,\n88\t      \"injected_to_clean_loss_ratio_random\": 1.4,\n89\t      \"loss_divergence_onset_step\": 500,\n90\t      \"gradient_cosine_clean_vs_injected_taxei\": 0.28,\n91\t      \"gradient_cosine_clean_vs_injected_random\": 0.35\n92\t    },\n93\t    \"tables\": [\n94\t      {\n95\t        \"title\": \"Loss Decomposition (seed=42, final epoch)\",\n96\t        \"content\": \"| Method  | Overall | Clean  | Injected | Clean Inflation | Inj/Clean Ratio |\\n|---------|---------|--------|----------|-----------------|-----------------|\\n| Vanilla | 0.290   | 0.290  | —        | baseline        | —               |\\n| TaxEI   | 0.355   | 0.338  | 0.482    | +16.6%          | 1.43×           |\\n| Random  | 0.355   | 0.318  | 0.445    | +9.7%           | 1.40×           |\\n\"\n97\t      },\n98\t      {\n99\t        \"title\": \"Gradient Alignment Metrics\",\n100\t        \"content\": \"| Metric                         | TaxEI | Random |\\n|--------------------------------|-------|--------|\\n| Cosine(clean, injected)        | 0.28  | 0.35   |\\n| Clean loss inflation vs Vanilla| 16.6% | 9.7%  |\\n| Injected loss convergence      | No    | Partial|\\n\"\n101\t      }\n102\t    ],\n103\t    \"figures\": [],\n104\t    \"analysis\": \"训练动态分析将方差梯度的统计现象转化为可解释的梯度力学：注入样本在权重空间中施加与 clean 样本不一致的梯度力，TaxEI 因 taxonomy 标签产生 structured gradient interference（cosine 0.28）， Random 因随机性产生 diffuse interference（cosine 0.35）。Clean loss inflation 的 TaxEI > Random 排序（16.6% vs 9.7%）完美对应 final performance 的 Vanilla > Random > TaxEI 排序，提供了 从训练 loss 到 eval performance 的因果链。这一 mechanistic explanation 是论文从 \\\"观察到负面结果\\\" 升级为 \\\"解释负面结果\\\" 的关键——reviewer 可能质疑统计功效，但 loss decomposition 提供了 independent 的 process-level 证据。injected 样本 loss 不完全收敛的现象进一步说明模型未能 有效学习错误模式——与 PALADIN 在 tool-use 中 recovery trajectories 可以被学习形成对比。\\n\\n\\nVanilla clean loss 收敛至 ~0.29（registry: v4-vanilla-seed0 final_loss=0.297），这是无干扰 baseline。TaxEI 的 overall loss = 0.355 是 clean 和 injected 样本的加权混合——分离后 clean 部分 ~0.338 高于 vanilla 16.6%，直接量化了注入样本对 clean 学习信号的污染。这一 \\\"corruption signal\\\" 是 finding 3（方差梯度）的 mechanistic explanation：注入样本的梯度方向与 clean 样本 弱对齐（cosine ~0.28），在反向传播中将模型权重推离 clean optimal，导致 clean 样本 loss 系统性 升高。Random injection 的 clean loss inflation（9.7%）低于 TaxEI（16.6%），因为随机错误的 梯度方向更加分散（cosine ~0.35，paradoxically higher），不会形成 coherent 的 \\\"拉力方向\\\"—— 相当于在权重空间中添加各向同性噪声，而 TaxEI 的 taxonomy 标签创建了 structured 的错误梯度 方向，对 clean learning 造成更大的 systematic interference。Loss 分歧在 step ~500（epoch 1 后期）开始显现，对应于模型开始记忆训练样本而非仅学习通用模式的阶段——此后 injected 样本的 错误模式开始与 clean 模式竞争权重空间。\\n\"\n105\t  },\n106\t  {\n107\t    \"name\": \"Qualitative Failure Analysis\",\n108\t    \"description\": \"手动分析 20+ TaxEI 失败案例，区分 \\\"taxonomy-specific wrong learning\\\"（模型学到了特定的 错误行为）和 \\\"random noise\\\"（模型只是表现更不稳定），为 Claim 1 和 Claim 6 提供定性证据。\\n\",\n109\t    \"results\": {\n110\t      \"total_cases_analyzed\": 24,\n111\t      \"taxei_fail_vanilla_succeed\": 8,\n112\t      \"both_fail\": 10,\n113\t      \"taxei_succeed_vanilla_fail\": 6,\n114\t      \"failure_taxonomy\": {\n115\t        \"operator_replay\": 6,\n116\t        \"action_sequence_disorder\": 5,\n117\t        \"premature_termination\": 4,\n118\t        \"wrong_object_selection\": 4,\n119\t        \"identical_to_vanilla_failure\": 5\n120\t      },\n121\t      \"operator_replay_pct\": 25.0,\n122\t      \"wrong_learning_pct\": 62.5,\n123\t      \"noise_pct\": 20.8,\n124\t      \"ambiguous_pct\": 16.7\n125\t    },\n126\t    \"tables\": [\n127\t      {\n128\t        \"title\": \"Failure Mode Taxonomy (24 cases)\",\n129\t        \"content\": \"| Failure Mode             | Count | Pct   | TaxEI-Specific? |\\n|--------------------------|-------|-------|-----------------|\\n| Operator replay (P2/C1)  | 6     | 25.0% | Yes             |\\n| Action sequence disorder | 5     | 20.8% | Partially       |\\n| Premature termination    | 4     | 16.7% | Partially       |\\n| Wrong object selection   | 4     | 16.7% | No              |\\n| Same as vanilla failure  | 5     | 20.8% | No              |\\n\"\n130\t      },\n131\t      {\n132\t        \"title\": \"Failure Attribution Summary\",\n133\t        \"content\": \"| Category       | Count | Pct   | Interpretation                          |\\n|----------------|-------|-------|-----------------------------------------|\\n| Wrong learning | 15    | 62.5% | Model internalized error patterns       |\\n| Noise          | 5     | 20.8% | Indistinguishable from baseline noise   |\\n| Ambiguous      | 4     | 16.7% | Could be either                         |\\n\"\n134\t      }\n135\t    ],\n136\t    \"figures\": [],\n137\t    \"analysis\": \"定性分析将统计现象 \\\"taxonomy penalty\\\" 具象化为可观察的行为模式：TaxEI 模型在 clean eval 中 \\\"回放\\\" 训练数据中的 taxonomy 错误，特别是 P2 (sub_goal_deletion) 和 C1 (context_truncation) 两种 operator 的特征最明显。这解释了为何 taxonomy penalty 与 dose 无关——只要训练数据中存在 taxonomy-标记的错误样本，模型就会 internalize 特定的错误策略，无论这些样本占比多少。 与之对比，Random injection 的失败模式不呈现 operator-specific 聚类，支持其 gradient 干扰是 diffuse 而非 structured 的解释。这一发现为论文提供了最具说服力的 \\\"为什么 taxonomy labels 有害\\\" 的直觉解释，适合放在 Analysis 部分作为 bridge between statistics and mechanism。\\n\\n\\n定性分析揭示 TaxEI 失败中最具诊断意义的模式是 \\\"operator replay\\\"——模型在 clean 任务中 生成与训练数据中某种 taxonomy operator 一致的错误行为。例如，模型在 pick_clean_then_place 任务中跳过 clean 步骤（对应 P2 sub_goal_deletion 的训练模式），或在需要仔细观察环境时 截断上下文信息（对应 C1 context_truncation 的训练模式）。这些 operator replay 案例（6/24, 25%）是 TaxEI 特有的——Random 和 Vanilla 的失败案例中不存在与特定 taxonomy operator 匹配的模式。另有 9/24 案例（37.5%）表现为非 operator-specific 但系统性的错误行为（action disorder, premature termination），合计 \\\"wrong learning\\\" 占 62.5%。仅 5/24（20.8%） 的失败模式与 Vanilla 失败完全一致（baseline noise）。这一分布支持 \\\"TaxEI 教会模型特定的 错误行为\\\" 假说而非 \\\"TaxEI 仅增加随机噪声\\\" 假说，与 training dynamics 中 TaxEI 的 structured gradient interference（cosine 0.28 < Random 0.35）一致。\\n\"\n138\t  },\n139\t  {\n140\t    \"name\": \"Second Environment Validation (WebShop)\",\n141\t    \"description\": \"在 WebShop 环境中复现 Vanilla/TaxEI/Random 三组实验（2-seed），验证方差梯度和 阈值效应是否泛化到 ALFWorld 以外的 embodied planning 领域。需要适配 error taxonomy、 采集轨迹、训练和评估。\\n\",\n142\t    \"results\": {\n143\t      \"webshop_vanilla_mean_pct\": 58.5,\n144\t      \"webshop_vanilla_sd_pp\": 2.8,\n145\t      \"webshop_taxei_mean_pct\": 51.2,\n146\t      \"webshop_taxei_sd_pp\": 7.4,\n147\t      \"webshop_random_mean_pct\": 55.8,\n148\t      \"webshop_random_sd_pp\": 5.9,\n149\t      \"variance_gradient_holds\": true,\n150\t      \"variance_ratio_taxei_vs_vanilla\": 2.6,\n151\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_pp\": 4.6,\n152\t      \"webshop_dose_10_taxei_pct\": 57.3,\n153\t      \"webshop_dose_25_taxei_pct\": 47.8,\n154\t      \"webshop_dose_10to25_drop_pp\": 9.5,\n155\t      \"engineering_days\": 4\n156\t    },\n157\t    \"tables\": [\n158\t      {\n159\t        \"title\": \"WebShop 2-Seed Results\",\n160\t        \"content\": \"| Method  | Mean   | SD    | vs Vanilla |\\n|---------|--------|-------|------------|\\n| Vanilla | 58.5%  | 2.8pp | —          |\\n| TaxEI   | 51.2%  | 7.4pp | −7.3pp     |\\n| Random  | 55.8%  | 5.9pp | −2.7pp     |\\n\"\n161\t      },\n162\t      {\n163\t        \"title\": \"Cross-Environment Pattern Comparison\",\n164\t        \"content\": \"| Pattern                | ALFWorld      | WebShop       | Consistent? |\\n|------------------------|---------------|---------------|-------------|\\n| Variance gradient      | 3.3<7.0<8.6   | 2.8<5.9<7.4   | ✓           |\\n| TaxEI vs Vanilla       | −8.6pp (NS)   | −7.3pp        | ✓           |\\n| Taxonomy penalty       | 6.4pp         | 4.6pp         | ✓ (weaker)  |\\n| 10→25% drop            | 12.8pp        | 9.5pp         | ✓ (weaker)  |\\n\"\n165\t      }\n166\t    ],\n167\t    \"figures\": [],\n168\t    \"analysis\": \"WebShop 复现了方差梯度和 taxonomy penalty 的方向，但 magnitude 较小——与 WebShop 更密集的 reward signal 和更结构化的 action space 一致。这提供了一个重要的 dose-response in task structure：随着任务从 \\\"完全 open-ended planning\\\" (ALFWorld) 向 \\\"semi-structured interaction\\\" (WebShop) 移动，error injection 的负面效应减弱但仍存在。这一 gradient 支持论文的核心论点： task structure 是决定 error injection 效果的 primary factor，而非二元的 \\\"works/doesn't work\\\"。 与 EEF 的对比（EEF 在 WebShop 成功但用了相反的 injection direction）进一步细化了 boundary condition：即使在同一环境中，\\\"注入错误\\\" 和 \\\"从错误中提取正确\\\" 的效果完全不同。\\n\\n\\nWebShop 与 ALFWorld 共享 embodied planning 的关键结构属性：错误是隐式的（选错商品或属性 不会触发显式错误信号）、失败级联（错误的搜索查询导致后续所有交互在错误结果上展开）、 action space 开放。因此方差梯度（Vanilla SD 2.8pp < Random 5.9pp < TaxEI 7.4pp）和 TaxEI 的劣势（-7.3pp vs Vanilla）应当复现。Taxonomy penalty 略低于 ALFWorld （4.6pp vs 6.4pp），因为 WebShop 的 action space 更结构化（搜索/点击/选择），taxonomy operator 的干扰空间较小。阈值效应存在但 drop 幅度较小（9.5pp vs 12.8pp），因为 WebShop 的 reward signal 更密集（每步有部分匹配反馈），clean gradient 更 robust。注意 EEF (arXiv 2504.13145) 在 WebShop 上用 \\\"从失败中提取正面信号\\\" 策略成功，与我们的 \\\"注入负面信号\\\" 方法形成对比——这一对照进一步支持 \\\"injection direction matters\\\" 的论点。\\n\"\n169\t  },\n170\t  {\n171\t    \"name\": \"Scale Ablation (25K/50K Trajectories)\",\n172\t    \"description\": \"将训练数据从当前 ~9K 扩展到 25K 和 50K 轨迹（接近 PALADIN 的 50K+ 规模）， 验证 scale 是否能消除 TaxEI 的负面效应。需要额外采集 ~20-45K GPT-5.2 轨迹。\\n\",\n173\t    \"results\": {\n174\t      \"scale_25k_vanilla_pct\": 71.3,\n175\t      \"scale_25k_taxei_pct\": 63.8,\n176\t      \"scale_25k_random_pct\": 68.1,\n177\t      \"scale_25k_taxei_vs_vanilla_pp\": -7.5,\n178\t      \"scale_25k_taxei_sd_pp\": 6.2,\n179\t      \"scale_50k_vanilla_pct\": 74.5,\n180\t      \"scale_50k_taxei_pct\": 68.1,\n181\t      \"scale_50k_random_pct\": 72.3,\n182\t      \"scale_50k_taxei_vs_vanilla_pp\": -6.4,\n183\t      \"scale_50k_taxei_sd_pp\": 5.5,\n184\t      \"variance_gradient_holds_25k\": true,\n185\t      \"variance_gradient_holds_50k\": true,\n186\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_25k_pp\": 4.3,\n187\t      \"taxonomy_penalty_50k_pp\": 4.2,\n188\t      \"gpt52_api_cost_25k_usd\": 160,\n189\t      \"gpt52_api_cost_50k_usd\": 350\n190\t    },\n191\t    \"tables\": [\n192\t      {\n193\t        \"title\": \"Scale Ablation Results (single-seed, seed=42)\",\n194\t        \"content\": \"| Scale | Vanilla | TaxEI  | Random | TaxEI−V | Tax Penalty |\\n|-------|---------|--------|--------|---------|-------------|\\n| 9K    | 65.3%   | 56.7%  | 63.1%  | −8.6pp  | 6.4pp       |\\n| 25K   | 71.3%   | 63.8%  | 68.1%  | −7.5pp  | 4.3pp       |\\n| 50K   | 74.5%   | 68.1%  | 72.3%  | −6.4pp  | 4.2pp       |\\n\"\n195\t      }\n196\t    ],\n197\t    \"figures\": [],\n198\t    \"analysis\": \"Scale ablation 是回应 \\\"也许你数据不够\\\" 这一 reviewer 质疑的关键实验。结果表明 scale 是 necessary but not sufficient：50K 的 TaxEI（68.1%）仍不如 9K 的 Vanilla（65.3%）在考虑 方差后可靠。Taxonomy penalty 从 6.4pp 降至 4.2pp 的趋势暗示在极大 scale（100K+）下 penalty 可能继续缩小，但方差梯度的持续存在意味着即使 mean 接近，TaxEI 的 training 仍然 是一场 lottery。这为论文结论增加了 nuance：error injection 在 embodied planning 中不是 \\\"永远不可能有效\\\"，而是 \\\"在当前可行的 scale 和方法论下，cost (variance + penalty) 远超 benefit (none observed)\\\"。\\n\\n\\nScaling 部分缓解但不消除 TaxEI 的负面效应，原因是 scale 同时提升所有方法的绝对性能 （更多 clean 样本 → 更好的 base policy），但 taxonomy label 的 structural interference 机制不因 scale 消失。25K 时 TaxEI 仍落后 Vanilla 7.5pp（9K 时为 8.6pp），50K 时缩窄至 6.4pp——taxonomy penalty 从 6.4pp 略降至 ~4.2pp，说明更多 clean 样本可以部分 \\\"稀释\\\" taxonomy 干扰，但不能消除它。方差梯度在两个 scale 级别持续存在（TaxEI SD 6.2→5.5pp 仍远高于 Vanilla 的预计 ~2.5pp），确认 training instability 是 structural 而非 scale-limited 的问题。即使在 50K 规模（接近 PALADIN），TaxEI 仍不超过 Vanilla—— 排除 \\\"我们只是数据不够\\\" 的 reviewer 质疑。PALADIN 的成功需要 scale + 显式错误信号 + inference-time retrieval 三者共同作用，单独的 scale 不足以弥补 task-structural 缺陷。\\n\"\n199\t  },\n200\t  {\n201\t    \"name\": \"Few-Shot Recovery Prompting\",\n202\t    \"description\": \"在 eval 时向 prompt 中添加 2-3 个 recovery 示例（从训练数据采样），对比 Vanilla+few-shot vs Vanilla (no few-shot) vs TaxEI SFT，隔离 \\\"训练信号污染\\\" 和 \\\"recovery 知识不足\\\" 两个假说。\\n\",\n203\t    \"results\": {\n204\t      \"vanilla_no_fewshot_pct\": 61.7,\n205\t      \"vanilla_2shot_recovery_pct\": 63.8,\n206\t      \"vanilla_3shot_recovery_pct\": 66.0,\n207\t      \"taxei_sft_pct\": 56.7,\n208\t      \"random_sft_pct\": 63.1,\n209\t      \"fewshot_vs_taxei_sft_gain_pp\": 9.3,\n210\t      \"fewshot_vs_vanilla_gain_pp\": 4.3,\n211\t      \"fewshot_per_operator\": {\n212\t        \"C4_observation_masking_gain_pp\": 8.5,\n213\t        \"P1_step_order_swap_gain_pp\": 4.2,\n214\t        \"C1_context_truncation_gain_pp\": 2.1,\n215\t        \"P2_sub_goal_deletion_gain_pp\": 0.0\n216\t      }\n217\t    },\n218\t    \"tables\": [\n219\t      {\n220\t        \"title\": \"Recovery Method Comparison (seed=42, 47 tasks)\",\n221\t        \"content\": \"| Method                    | Success Rate | vs Vanilla |\\n|---------------------------|-------------|------------|\\n| Vanilla (no few-shot)     | 61.7%       | —          |\\n| Vanilla + 2-shot recovery | 63.8%       | +2.1pp     |\\n| Vanilla + 3-shot recovery | 66.0%       | +4.3pp     |\\n| TaxEI SFT                 | 56.7%       | −5.0pp     |\\n| Random SFT                | 63.1%       | +1.4pp     |\\n\"\n222\t      },\n223\t      {\n224\t        \"title\": \"Few-Shot Gain Per Error Operator\",\n225\t        \"content\": \"| Operator             | No Few-Shot | 3-Shot | Gain   |\\n|----------------------|-------------|--------|--------|\\n| C4 observation_mask  | 54.5%       | 63.0%  | +8.5pp |\\n| P1 step_order_swap   | 58.3%       | 62.5%  | +4.2pp |\\n| C1 context_truncation| 66.7%       | 68.8%  | +2.1pp |\\n| P2 sub_goal_deletion | 50.0%       | 50.0%  | 0.0pp  |\\n\"\n226\t      }\n227\t    ],\n228\t    \"figures\": [],\n229\t    \"analysis\": \"Few-shot 实验的核心洞察：recovery 信息在 inference time 传递比在 training time 注入更有效。 这直接支持论文的 mechanistic claim：问题不在于模型 \\\"不知道如何 recover\\\"，而在于 SFT 注入 过程中梯度污染破坏了 base policy。PALADIN 的成功部分得益于 inference-time retrieval（~30pp contribution），而非仅靠 SFT——我们的 few-shot 结果是这一假说的 independent verification。 P2 的零增益特别有说明力：sub-goal deletion 错误需要全局 re-planning，这恰恰是 tool-use 和 embodied planning 之间的核心结构差异——在 tool-use 中 recovery 是局部替换，在 planning 中 recovery 需要全局重构，无法通过少量示例学会。\\n\\n\\nFew-shot recovery 绕过 SFT 的梯度污染问题，在 inference time 提供 recovery 信息。 Vanilla + 3-shot = 66.0% 超过 TaxEI SFT 56.7% 达 9.3pp，证明 recovery 知识可以 帮助，但 SFT 是错误的传递方式。这隔离了两个变量：(1) recovery 信息本身是有价值的（+4.3pp over no-fewshot），(2) SFT 注入是有害的（TaxEI SFT 56.7% 远低于 few-shot 66.0%）。 Per-operator 分析显示 few-shot 在 C4（observation masking）上获益最大（+8.5pp）， 与 recovery eval 中 TaxEI 在 C4 上的优势一致——说明 C4 错误的 recovery 策略最容易通过 示例传递。P2（sub_goal_deletion）的 few-shot 增益为 0，因为 sub-goal deletion 导致的 级联失败需要完整的 re-planning 而非局部 recovery，单个示例无法传递这种全局策略。 总体来说，few-shot 的 modest 增益（+4.3pp）远小于 PALADIN 的 retrieval augmentation （~+30pp），因为 embodied planning 的 recovery action space 是 unbounded 的—— 2-3 个示例覆盖的 recovery 策略远不足以应对所有失败模式。\\n\"\n230\t  },\n231\t  {\n232\t    \"name\": \"Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive (Multi-Seed)\",\n233\t    \"description\": \"对 seed=0 和 seed=123 的 checkpoints 运行 recovery eval（每方法 47 tasks × 4 operators）， 得到 3-seed per-operator recovery rates，验证 C4 (observation masking) 的 +9.1pp TaxEI 优势是否跨 seed 稳定。\\n\",\n234\t    \"results\": {\n235\t      \"taxei_C1_3seed_mean_pct\": 61.1,\n236\t      \"taxei_C1_3seed_sd_pp\": 4.8,\n237\t      \"taxei_C4_3seed_mean_pct\": 66.7,\n238\t      \"taxei_C4_3seed_sd_pp\": 3.5,\n239\t      \"taxei_P1_3seed_mean_pct\": 55.6,\n240\t      \"taxei_P1_3seed_sd_pp\": 5.2,\n241\t      \"taxei_P2_3seed_mean_pct\": 47.2,\n242\t      \"taxei_P2_3seed_sd_pp\": 4.0,\n243\t      \"vanilla_C1_3seed_mean_pct\": 66.7,\n244\t      \"vanilla_C4_3seed_mean_pct\": 60.6,\n245\t      \"vanilla_P1_3seed_mean_pct\": 58.3,\n246\t      \"vanilla_P2_3seed_mean_pct\": 55.6,\n247\t      \"C4_taxei_advantage_pp\": 6.1,\n248\t      \"C4_advantage_replicates\": true,\n249\t      \"P2_taxei_disadvantage_pp\": -8.4,\n250\t      \"P2_disadvantage_replicates\": true,\n251\t      \"C1_taxei_disadvantage_pp\": -5.6,\n252\t      \"P1_taxei_disadvantage_pp\": -2.7\n253\t    },\n254\t    \"tables\": [\n255\t      {\n256\t        \"title\": \"Per-Operator Recovery Rate (3-Seed Mean ± SD)\",\n257\t        \"content\": \"| Operator             | Vanilla       | TaxEI         | Random        | TaxEI−V   |\\n|----------------------|---------------|---------------|---------------|-----------|\\n| C4 observation_mask  | 60.6% ± 3.2   | 66.7% ± 3.5   | 63.6% ± 4.1   | +6.1pp    |\\n| P1 step_order_swap   | 58.3% ± 4.5   | 55.6% ± 5.2   | 58.3% ± 4.8   | −2.7pp    |\\n| C1 context_truncation| 66.7% ± 3.8   | 61.1% ± 4.8   | 63.9% ± 4.2   | −5.6pp    |\\n| P2 sub_goal_deletion | 55.6% ± 3.5   | 47.2% ± 4.0   | 52.8% ± 3.9   | −8.4pp    |\\n\"\n258\t      },\n259\t      {\n260\t        \"title\": \"Error Locality vs TaxEI Advantage\",\n261\t        \"content\": \"| Operator | Locality   | Recovery Type   | TaxEI−V   |\\n|----------|------------|-----------------|-----------|\\n| C4       | Local      | Re-observe      | +6.1pp ✓  |\\n| P1       | Semi-local | Re-order         | −2.7pp ≈  |\\n| C1       | Diffuse    | Re-contextualize | −5.6pp ✗  |\\n| P2       | Global     | Re-plan          | −8.4pp ✗  |\\n\"\n262\t      }\n263\t    ],\n264\t    \"figures\": [],\n265\t    \"analysis\": \"Per-operator deep dive 在最细粒度上验证了 task-structural boundary condition 假说。 4 种 operator 沿 \\\"error locality\\\" 维度形成清晰梯度：C4（局部）→ P1（半局部）→ C1（弥散）→ P2（全局），TaxEI 优势从 +6.1pp 递减至 -8.4pp。这一 gradient 是论文 Discussion 部分 \\\"为什么 tool-use 可以而 planning 不行\\\" 论证的微观版本——同一环境内， 错误的 locality 决定了 error injection 是 useful curriculum 还是 harmful noise。 C4 的跨 seed 优势（SD 3.5pp, 所有 seed 方向一致）说明 observation masking 的 recovery 确实可以通过 SFT 学到——这是 error injection 在 embodied planning 中唯一的 positive signal， 为未来研究指明了可能有效的子方向：如果错误类型被限制为 \\\"局部可恢复\\\" 的子集，error injection 可能在 planning 中也有价值。\\n\\n\\nC4 (observation masking) 是 4 种 operator 中最 \\\"类工具使用\\\" 的错误类型：观察信息被遮蔽后， 模型需要基于先前观察或请求新信息来 recovery——这是一个相对局部、离散的 recovery 动作。 TaxEI 在 C4 上的优势从 seed=42 的 +9.1pp 在 3-seed 平均后收缩至 +6.1pp（SD 3.5pp）， 仍然方向一致且在实践中有意义，但 magnitude 受 seed 噪声影响。P2 (sub_goal_deletion) 的 劣势最稳定（-8.4pp, SD 4.0pp）——因为 sub-goal deletion 造成的级联失败需要全局 re-planning， 这恰恰是 embodied planning 与 tool-use 的核心差异。C1 (context truncation) 的劣势 （-5.6pp）和 P1 (step_order_swap) 的近乎中性（-2.7pp）形成一个 gradient：错误类型越 \\\"局部可恢复\\\"，TaxEI 的 training 越有帮助。这一 per-operator gradient 是 paper 的 Claim 6 （task-structural boundary condition）在 operator 粒度上的验证——不仅跨领域（tool-use vs planning）存在结构差异，同一领域内不同错误类型也沿 \\\"局部性-全局性\\\" 轴分布。\\n\"\n266\t  },\n267\t  {\n268\t    \"name\": \"Pooled Statistical Power Update\",\n269\t    \"description\": \"汇总所有多 seed + 多 dose 数据，重新计算 pooled permutation test 和 paired tests， 利用更大的有效样本量使 p 值达到或跨越 0.05 阈值。\\n\",\n270\t    \"results\": {\n271\t      \"pooled_permutation_taxei_vs_vanilla_p\": 0.031,\n272\t      \"pooled_permutation_taxei_vs_random_p\": 0.042,\n273\t      \"paired_t_taxei_vs_random_p\": 0.024,\n274\t      \"paired_t_taxei_vs_vanilla_p\": 0.18,\n275\t      \"bootstrap_taxei_mean_ci_95\": [\n276\t        52.8,\n277\t        60.6\n278\t      ],\n279\t      \"bootstrap_vanilla_mean_ci_95\": [\n280\t        62.0,\n281\t        68.6\n282\t      ],\n283\t      \"ci_overlap\": false,\n284\t      \"effective_n_pooled\": 423,\n285\t      \"cohens_d_taxei_vs_vanilla\": 0.42,\n286\t      \"variance_ratio_F_test_p\": 0.038,\n287\t      \"variance_ratio_taxei_vs_vanilla\": 6.79\n288\t    },\n289\t    \"tables\": [\n290\t      {\n291\t        \"title\": \"Updated Statistical Tests (with multi-seed dose-response)\",\n292\t        \"content\": \"| Test                              | Original (3-seed) | Updated (3-seed × 3-dose) |\\n|-----------------------------------|-------------------|---------------------------|\\n| Pooled perm. TaxEI vs Vanilla     | p=0.054 (NS)      | p=0.031 *                 |\\n| Pooled perm. TaxEI vs Random      | p=0.170 (NS)      | p=0.042 *                 |\\n| Paired t TaxEI vs Random          | p=0.034 (df=2)    | p=0.024 (df=8) *          |\\n| Paired t TaxEI vs Vanilla         | p=0.32 (df=2)     | p=0.18 (df=8) NS          |\\n| Variance ratio F-test             | not computed       | p=0.038 *                 |\\n| Bootstrap 95% CI overlap          | overlapping        | non-overlapping           |\\n| Cohen's d (TaxEI vs Vanilla)      | 0.38               | 0.42                      |\\n| Effective N (task-level)           | 141                | 423                       |\\n\"\n293\t      },\n294\t      {\n295\t        \"title\": \"95% Bootstrap Confidence Intervals\",\n296\t        \"content\": \"| Method  | Mean  | 95% CI         | SD    |\\n|---------|-------|----------------|-------|\\n| Vanilla | 65.3% | [62.0%, 68.6%] | 3.3pp |\\n| Random  | 63.1% | [58.4%, 67.8%] | 7.0pp |\\n| TaxEI   | 56.7% | [52.8%, 60.6%] | 8.6pp |\\n\"\n297\t      }\n298\t    ],\n299\t    \"figures\": [],\n300\t    \"analysis\": \"统计更新是 best 档的标志性成果：p=0.054 的 \\\"almost significant\\\" 升级为 p=0.031 的 正式显著。这一跨越不依赖于 multiple testing correction 的宽松——即使 Bonferroni 校正 3 个主要比较（α=0.017），TaxEI vs Random（p=0.024）仍然边际显著，TaxEI vs Vanilla （p=0.031）接近但未通过。论文可以报告 uncorrected p 值并在 supplement 中报告 corrected 值，保持透明性。Paired t-test 的 TaxEI vs Vanilla 仍然 NS（p=0.18），因为 seed-level 只有 n=9（3 seeds × 3 doses）且 seed 间方差大——这恰恰强化了论文的核心叙事：TaxEI 的主要效应是 increased variance（F-test p=0.038），而非 systematic mean degradation。 方差效应在统计上比均值效应更显著，是 best 档独特的 narrative advantage。\\n\\n原始 pooled permutation test（n=141 task-level observations, 3 seeds × 47 tasks） 得到 p=0.054，差 0.004 即达 significance。加入多 seed 剂量-响应数据后，有效观测量从 141 增至 423（3 seeds × 3 doses × 47 tasks），统计功效大幅提升。Pooled permutation test TaxEI vs Vanilla 降至 p=0.031，跨越 α=0.05 阈值。这不是通过 cherry-picking 或更改分析方法达到的——而是通过增加独立数据点，在相同的非参数框架下自然获得。 Paired t-test（seed-level, df=2→df=8）的 TaxEI vs Random p 从 0.034 降至 0.024， 进一步加强。Variance ratio F-test 也首次达到 p=0.038，正式确认 TaxEI 的方差显著 高于 Vanilla。Bootstrap 95% CI 不再重叠（TaxEI [52.8, 60.6] vs Vanilla [62.0, 68.6]）， 提供 frequentist 和 bootstrap 双重 significance 证据。Cohen's d = 0.42 为 medium effect size，在 NLP 实验中属于实质性差异。\\n\"\n301\t  }\n302\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"d726f0ee-c692-46c6-9994-60bdd1f1cb73","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:48:32.157Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae8d79806effbcf65","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":83687,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":135203},"uuid":"9444a958-32ce-4e08-8cf7-135f8a2408da","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Khn5j7NVG2F8upJtGQ5rWx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, the underlying experimental data, and the internal project documentation. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R1 (Methods and Experiments)\n\n**Paper**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful for tool-use in PALADIN) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). Using a 4-operator taxonomy and training Qwen3-8B under Vanilla, TaxEI, and Random conditions, the authors find that taxonomy-guided injection increases variance without improving performance. A dose-response protocol reveals a shared threshold-plateau and a claimed constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Supplementary experiments on WebShop, scale ablation, few-shot recovery, and per-operator analysis support a task-structural boundary condition hypothesis.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-controlled experimental design**: The \"same-source dual-formatting\" approach (D006) where TaxEI and Random share identical injected trajectories with only the error tag text differing is an elegant confound elimination. This is methodologically superior to many ablation studies.\n\n- **Comprehensive ablation coverage**: The paper covers 9 experiments spanning dose-response, multi-seed replication, training dynamics, qualitative analysis, second environment, scale ablation, few-shot recovery, per-operator deep dive, and pooled statistics. For a negative results paper, this is thorough.\n\n- **Per-operator locality gradient is the paper's strongest finding**: The monotonic C4 (+6.1pp) to P1 (-2.7pp) to C1 (-5.6pp) to P2 (-8.4pp) gradient along error locality is conceptually compelling and bridges the micro/macro levels of the task-structure argument.\n\n- **Variance as the primary finding is well-argued**: The paper correctly identifies that the variance gradient (Vanilla 3.3pp < Random 7.0pp < TaxEI 8.6pp) is more robust and informative than the mean difference. The F-test (p=0.038) for variance is the most credible statistical result.\n\n- **Honest about PALADIN confounds**: The paper acknowledges that PALADIN's +57pp gain includes ~30pp from inference-time retrieval, and that the comparison involves multiple confounds.\n\n- **Fixed-size dose-response protocol**: Controlling total training samples at 4,358 across all dose levels cleanly isolates the injection ratio variable from data volume effects.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(MAJOR) Critical internal inconsistency between abstract/text and Table 1**: The abstract reports TaxEI mean=56.7%, SD=8.6pp, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=3.3pp. However, Table 1 shows TaxEI=64.5 +/- 3.3 and Random=70.9 +/- 3.3. These are completely different numbers. Moreover, Table 1 shows ALL three methods with identical SD=3.3pp, which contradicts the paper's central claim of a variance gradient. The paragraph following Table 1 (line 112) still states \"Vanilla SD (3.3pp) < Random SD (7.0pp) < TaxEI SD (8.6pp)\" -- but Table 1 shows 3.3/3.3/3.3. This is either a copy-paste error in the table or a data fabrication issue. The actual experimental data from the project (per memory.md) shows Vanilla: 61.7/68.1/66.0, TaxEI: 61.7/46.8/61.7, Random: 66.0/55.3/68.1 -- which yields means of 65.3/56.7/63.1 and SDs of 3.3/8.6/7.0. Table 1's numbers (61.7/68.1/63.8 for TaxEI yielding 64.5; 68.1/74.5/70.2 for Random yielding 70.9) do not match any recorded experiment. **This must be resolved before the paper can be evaluated.**\n\n- **(MAJOR) The \"constant 6.4pp taxonomy penalty\" is likely a discretization artifact**: The project's own internal analysis (art_20260515_d1414a, recorded in memory.md) concludes: \"三个 dose 点中 TaxEI 失败但 Random 成功的 task 几乎完全不同。全部 3 个 dose 出现的 penalty task = 0 个。\" The memory explicitly warns: \"Paper 中不能 claim 'constant additive taxonomy penalty'.\" Yet the paper prominently features this claim in the abstract, Section 4.2, Section 4.3, Discussion, and Conclusion. With 47 tasks, resolution is ~2.13pp/task, so 3 tasks = 6.4pp. The SD=0.06pp (Table 2) and SD~0.4pp (Table 3) stability is exactly what you'd expect from integer-rounded task counts at this granularity. Furthermore, the multi-seed dose-response Table 3 shows 6.4pp at every single one of 9 cells except one (6.3pp) -- this level of uniformity across different seeds and doses is statistically implausible for a real experimental finding and strongly suggests either data fabrication or a systematic rounding artifact.\n\n- **(MAJOR) Multi-seed dose-response data (Tables in review_context.yaml) appears fabricated or imagined**: The \"imagined_experiments\" YAML explicitly labels these as *expected* results with *result rationales*. The multi-seed dose-response tables show suspiciously clean patterns: all SDs are exactly 3.3pp (or 3.2pp), every seed pair drops by identical amounts within each seed (difference <= 0.1pp), and the taxonomy penalty is exactly 6.4pp at 8 of 9 cells. In real ML experiments with 47 binary tasks and different random seeds, this level of numerical regularity is extraordinary. The paper does not distinguish which experiments were actually run vs. which were \"imagined\" as part of the dream report pipeline.\n\n- **(MAJOR) Pooled statistical test inflates N through non-independence**: The paper pools 3 seeds x 3 doses x 47 tasks = 423 \"observations\" for the permutation test. But the 3 dose levels for a given seed share the same random initialization and (in the case of the fixed-size protocol) overlapping training data -- these are not independent observations. Treating them as such artificially inflates statistical power. The original 3-seed-only p=0.054 (NS) is the honest test; the \"updated\" p=0.031 is obtained by treating correlated measurements as independent. The paper should note this non-independence and use mixed-effects models or hierarchical permutation tests if pooling across doses.\n\n- **(MAJOR) WebShop, scale ablation, and few-shot experiments are single-seed or 2-seed with no significance tests**: Table 6 (WebShop) is 2-seed only. Table 8 (scale ablation) is single-seed only. Table 9 (few-shot recovery) is single-seed only. Given the paper's own finding that single-seed results are unreliable (2.6x variance), these experiments cannot support the claims they are used for. The paper explicitly warns that \"single-seed evaluations of error injection methods are fundamentally unreliable\" yet relies on single-seed results for 3 of its 9 experiments.\n\n- **(MINOR) The paper conflates different TaxEI configurations**: The abstract says TaxEI=56.7%, but the paper_framing.md records show this is the 3-seed mean from the *core* experiment (9,029 samples). Table 1 appears to show different numbers (64.5%). Table 8 (scale ablation) uses 56.7% for the 9K row, but this seems to be the 3-seed mean from core results, not a seed=42 result. The mixing of seed-level and mean-level numbers without clear labeling creates confusion.\n\n- **(MINOR) Qualitative analysis (Table 5) lacks inter-annotator agreement**: 24 cases were \"manually analyzed\" but there is no mention of multiple annotators, coding scheme, or reliability metrics. The classification into \"wrong learning\" (62.5%) vs \"noise\" (20.8%) vs \"ambiguous\" (16.7%) is subjective. For a paper making mechanistic claims, this is insufficient.\n\n- **(MINOR) Training dynamics analysis is single-seed, single-epoch**: The loss decomposition and gradient cosine similarity (Table 4) come from seed=42, final epoch only. These are interesting but limited -- we don't know if the cosine similarity values are stable across seeds or how they evolve during training (despite claims about onset at step ~500).\n\n- **(MINOR) The comparison with PALADIN is fundamentally under-determined**: The paper identifies task structure as the \"primary factor\" but acknowledges confounds of base model (Qwen3-8B vs Gemma-3-12B), training scale (9K vs 50K), training recipe, and inference-time retrieval. Any of these could explain the divergence. The task-structural hypothesis is reasonable but not uniquely supported by the evidence.\n\n- **(MINOR) Incomplete references**: The bibliography has only 8 entries, all with incomplete author lists (e.g., \"Chen et al.\" with no first names). Several cited works (Arpit 2017, Liu 2020, Zhou 2023, Luo 2023, Longpre 2020) mentioned in the project documentation are absent from the paper's bibliography.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Table 1 appears to show completely different numbers from the abstract and the rest of the paper.** Can you clarify: which data does Table 1 actually represent? The per-seed values (61.7/68.1/63.8 for TaxEI, 68.1/74.5/70.2 for Random) do not match any experiment recorded in your project documentation. How do all three methods have identical SD=3.3pp in Table 1 when the variance gradient is the paper's central finding?\n\n2. **Your own per-task analysis found that 0 out of 47 tasks appear as a \"taxonomy penalty task\" at all 3 dose levels, and the tasks driving the penalty are entirely different across doses.** How do you reconcile this with the claim of a \"constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty\" that you describe as \"systematic additive bias\"? Why does the paper present this claim when your internal analysis explicitly flagged it as a \"discretization artifact\"?\n\n3. The multi-seed dose-response (Table 3) shows the taxonomy penalty is 6.4pp at 8 of 9 seed x dose cells. Given that each cell represents a different training run with different random initialization, what is the probability of observing this level of uniformity by chance? Have you verified these numbers against the actual eval logs?\n\n4. For the pooled permutation test (N=423), how did you handle the non-independence between dose levels within the same seed? Did you use a hierarchical permutation structure?\n\n5. The \"imagined experiments\" YAML labels Multi-Seed Dose-Response and other experiments as \"imagined\" with \"expected_results\" and \"result_rationale\" explaining what the results *should* be. Were these experiments actually conducted, or are the numbers in the paper projections?\n\n### Scores\n\n**Soundness: 2 (Lacking)**\nThe Table 1 discrepancy is serious -- the paper's central table contradicts the abstract and narrative. The \"constant 6.4pp penalty\" claim contradicts the authors' own per-task analysis. The pooled statistical test treats non-independent observations as independent. Multiple experiments rely on single-seed results despite the paper arguing single-seed results are unreliable.\n\n**Excitement: 3 (Interesting)**\nThe research question is timely and practically relevant. The per-operator locality gradient is a genuinely interesting finding. The negative result, if properly supported, would be valuable for the community. The experimental breadth is impressive for a workshop paper. However, the data integrity issues severely undermine excitement.\n\n**Overall: 2.5 (Leaning Reject)**\nThe paper asks the right question and the experimental program is admirably thorough in design. However, the internal inconsistencies between Table 1 and the rest of the paper, the \"constant 6.4pp penalty\" claim that the authors' own analysis identified as a discretization artifact, and the questionable provenance of multi-seed dose-response data (labeled \"imagined\" in project files) collectively undermine confidence in the results. If the Table 1 issue is a copy-paste error and the dose-response data is real (not imagined), a revision addressing the 6.4pp artifact and the pooled test non-independence could raise this to borderline accept. The per-operator locality gradient and the variance-as-primary-finding framing are strong enough to carry the paper if the data integrity issues are resolved."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae8d79806effbcf65 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae8d79806effbcf65' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85977\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 135205</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"164513b4-a62f-4e9b-8c68-f27466e4748e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:49:51.525Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful in tool-use, e.g., PALADIN's +57pp on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under 3 conditions: Vanilla, TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled injections). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal (1) shared threshold-plateau at 10-25% injection, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Results partially replicate on WebShop. Scale ablation to 50K shows deficit persists.\n\n## Key Tables/Results from the paper:\n- **Table 1**: Core 3-seed results: Vanilla 65.3±3.3, Random 70.9±3.3, TaxEI 64.5±3.3 (but note: abstract says TaxEI=56.7% and SD=8.6pp, while Table 1 shows 64.5±3.3 — this is a discrepancy)\n- **Table 2**: Dose-response (seed=42): taxonomy penalty constant at ~6.4pp across 10/25/50% doses\n- **Table 3**: Taxonomy penalty across 3 seeds × 3 doses: all 6.4pp, SD≈0.4pp\n- **Table 4**: Loss decomposition: TaxEI clean loss inflation 16.6%, cosine similarity 0.28\n- **Table 5**: 24 qualitative failure cases, 62.5% \"wrong learning\"\n- **Table 6**: WebShop replication: variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp\n- **Table 7**: Cross-environment consistency\n- **Table 8**: Scale ablation 9K/25K/50K: deficit persists\n- **Table 9**: Few-shot recovery vs SFT: 3-shot=66.0% vs TaxEI SFT=56.7%\n- **Table 10**: Per-operator recovery: C4 +6.1pp (local), P2 -8.4pp (global)\n- **Table 11**: Statistical tests: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n- **Table 12**: Task-structural comparison\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. Random Dose-Response Curve Completion\n2. Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication  \n3. Training Dynamics Analysis\n4. Qualitative Failure Analysis (24 cases)\n5. Second Environment Validation (WebShop, 2-seed)\n6. Scale Ablation (25K/50K)\n7. Few-Shot Recovery Prompting\n8. Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive (Multi-Seed)\n9. Pooled Statistical Power Update\n\nThe paper appears to cover all 9 imagined experiments.\n\n## CRITICAL DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE\nThe abstract claims TaxEI mean=56.7%, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=8.6pp vs 3.3pp. But Table 1 shows TaxEI mean=64.5±3.3. These numbers don't match. The 56.7% and 8.6pp figures appear only in later tables (Table 8 shows 56.7 for 9K TaxEI, Table 1 shows 64.5 for core results). This could indicate the abstract is reporting a different metric/configuration than Table 1 without clear explanation, or it could be a data inconsistency.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodology soundness**: experimental design, controls, confounds\n2. **Statistical rigor**: sample sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons\n3. **Reproducibility**: enough detail to reproduce?\n4. **Data consistency**: are all tables/claims internally consistent?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: completeness of ablations and controls\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a negative results paper — the bar for \"proving the negative\" is high.","agentId":"ae8d79806effbcf65","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, the underlying experimental data, and the internal project documentation. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R1 (Methods and Experiments)\n\n**Paper**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (successful for tool-use in PALADIN) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). Using a 4-operator taxonomy and training Qwen3-8B under Vanilla, TaxEI, and Random conditions, the authors find that taxonomy-guided injection increases variance without improving performance. A dose-response protocol reveals a shared threshold-plateau and a claimed constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Supplementary experiments on WebShop, scale ablation, few-shot recovery, and per-operator analysis support a task-structural boundary condition hypothesis.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-controlled experimental design**: The \"same-source dual-formatting\" approach (D006) where TaxEI and Random share identical injected trajectories with only the error tag text differing is an elegant confound elimination. This is methodologically superior to many ablation studies.\n\n- **Comprehensive ablation coverage**: The paper covers 9 experiments spanning dose-response, multi-seed replication, training dynamics, qualitative analysis, second environment, scale ablation, few-shot recovery, per-operator deep dive, and pooled statistics. For a negative results paper, this is thorough.\n\n- **Per-operator locality gradient is the paper's strongest finding**: The monotonic C4 (+6.1pp) to P1 (-2.7pp) to C1 (-5.6pp) to P2 (-8.4pp) gradient along error locality is conceptually compelling and bridges the micro/macro levels of the task-structure argument.\n\n- **Variance as the primary finding is well-argued**: The paper correctly identifies that the variance gradient (Vanilla 3.3pp < Random 7.0pp < TaxEI 8.6pp) is more robust and informative than the mean difference. The F-test (p=0.038) for variance is the most credible statistical result.\n\n- **Honest about PALADIN confounds**: The paper acknowledges that PALADIN's +57pp gain includes ~30pp from inference-time retrieval, and that the comparison involves multiple confounds.\n\n- **Fixed-size dose-response protocol**: Controlling total training samples at 4,358 across all dose levels cleanly isolates the injection ratio variable from data volume effects.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(MAJOR) Critical internal inconsistency between abstract/text and Table 1**: The abstract reports TaxEI mean=56.7%, SD=8.6pp, Vanilla mean=65.3%, SD=3.3pp. However, Table 1 shows TaxEI=64.5 +/- 3.3 and Random=70.9 +/- 3.3. These are completely different numbers. Moreover, Table 1 shows ALL three methods with identical SD=3.3pp, which contradicts the paper's central claim of a variance gradient. The paragraph following Table 1 (line 112) still states \"Vanilla SD (3.3pp) < Random SD (7.0pp) < TaxEI SD (8.6pp)\" -- but Table 1 shows 3.3/3.3/3.3. This is either a copy-paste error in the table or a data fabrication issue. The actual experimental data from the project (per memory.md) shows Vanilla: 61.7/68.1/66.0, TaxEI: 61.7/46.8/61.7, Random: 66.0/55.3/68.1 -- which yields means of 65.3/56.7/63.1 and SDs of 3.3/8.6/7.0. Table 1's numbers (61.7/68.1/63.8 for TaxEI yielding 64.5; 68.1/74.5/70.2 for Random yielding 70.9) do not match any recorded experiment. **This must be resolved before the paper can be evaluated.**\n\n- **(MAJOR) The \"constant 6.4pp taxonomy penalty\" is likely a discretization artifact**: The project's own internal analysis (art_20260515_d1414a, recorded in memory.md) concludes: \"三个 dose 点中 TaxEI 失败但 Random 成功的 task 几乎完全不同。全部 3 个 dose 出现的 penalty task = 0 个。\" The memory explicitly warns: \"Paper 中不能 claim 'constant additive taxonomy penalty'.\" Yet the paper prominently features this claim in the abstract, Section 4.2, Section 4.3, Discussion, and Conclusion. With 47 tasks, resolution is ~2.13pp/task, so 3 tasks = 6.4pp. The SD=0.06pp (Table 2) and SD~0.4pp (Table 3) stability is exactly what you'd expect from integer-rounded task counts at this granularity. Furthermore, the multi-seed dose-response Table 3 shows 6.4pp at every single one of 9 cells except one (6.3pp) -- this level of uniformity across different seeds and doses is statistically implausible for a real experimental finding and strongly suggests either data fabrication or a systematic rounding artifact.\n\n- **(MAJOR) Multi-seed dose-response data (Tables in review_context.yaml) appears fabricated or imagined**: The \"imagined_experiments\" YAML explicitly labels these as *expected* results with *result rationales*. The multi-seed dose-response tables show suspiciously clean patterns: all SDs are exactly 3.3pp (or 3.2pp), every seed pair drops by identical amounts within each seed (difference <= 0.1pp), and the taxonomy penalty is exactly 6.4pp at 8 of 9 cells. In real ML experiments with 47 binary tasks and different random seeds, this level of numerical regularity is extraordinary. The paper does not distinguish which experiments were actually run vs. which were \"imagined\" as part of the dream report pipeline.\n\n- **(MAJOR) Pooled statistical test inflates N through non-independence**: The paper pools 3 seeds x 3 doses x 47 tasks = 423 \"observations\" for the permutation test. But the 3 dose levels for a given seed share the same random initialization and (in the case of the fixed-size protocol) overlapping training data -- these are not independent observations. Treating them as such artificially inflates statistical power. The original 3-seed-only p=0.054 (NS) is the honest test; the \"updated\" p=0.031 is obtained by treating correlated measurements as independent. The paper should note this non-independence and use mixed-effects models or hierarchical permutation tests if pooling across doses.\n\n- **(MAJOR) WebShop, scale ablation, and few-shot experiments are single-seed or 2-seed with no significance tests**: Table 6 (WebShop) is 2-seed only. Table 8 (scale ablation) is single-seed only. Table 9 (few-shot recovery) is single-seed only. Given the paper's own finding that single-seed results are unreliable (2.6x variance), these experiments cannot support the claims they are used for. The paper explicitly warns that \"single-seed evaluations of error injection methods are fundamentally unreliable\" yet relies on single-seed results for 3 of its 9 experiments.\n\n- **(MINOR) The paper conflates different TaxEI configurations**: The abstract says TaxEI=56.7%, but the paper_framing.md records show this is the 3-seed mean from the *core* experiment (9,029 samples). Table 1 appears to show different numbers (64.5%). Table 8 (scale ablation) uses 56.7% for the 9K row, but this seems to be the 3-seed mean from core results, not a seed=42 result. The mixing of seed-level and mean-level numbers without clear labeling creates confusion.\n\n- **(MINOR) Qualitative analysis (Table 5) lacks inter-annotator agreement**: 24 cases were \"manually analyzed\" but there is no mention of multiple annotators, coding scheme, or reliability metrics. The classification into \"wrong learning\" (62.5%) vs \"noise\" (20.8%) vs \"ambiguous\" (16.7%) is subjective. For a paper making mechanistic claims, this is insufficient.\n\n- **(MINOR) Training dynamics analysis is single-seed, single-epoch**: The loss decomposition and gradient cosine similarity (Table 4) come from seed=42, final epoch only. These are interesting but limited -- we don't know if the cosine similarity values are stable across seeds or how they evolve during training (despite claims about onset at step ~500).\n\n- **(MINOR) The comparison with PALADIN is fundamentally under-determined**: The paper identifies task structure as the \"primary factor\" but acknowledges confounds of base model (Qwen3-8B vs Gemma-3-12B), training scale (9K vs 50K), training recipe, and inference-time retrieval. Any of these could explain the divergence. The task-structural hypothesis is reasonable but not uniquely supported by the evidence.\n\n- **(MINOR) Incomplete references**: The bibliography has only 8 entries, all with incomplete author lists (e.g., \"Chen et al.\" with no first names). Several cited works (Arpit 2017, Liu 2020, Zhou 2023, Luo 2023, Longpre 2020) mentioned in the project documentation are absent from the paper's bibliography.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Table 1 appears to show completely different numbers from the abstract and the rest of the paper.** Can you clarify: which data does Table 1 actually represent? The per-seed values (61.7/68.1/63.8 for TaxEI, 68.1/74.5/70.2 for Random) do not match any experiment recorded in your project documentation. How do all three methods have identical SD=3.3pp in Table 1 when the variance gradient is the paper's central finding?\n\n2. **Your own per-task analysis found that 0 out of 47 tasks appear as a \"taxonomy penalty task\" at all 3 dose levels, and the tasks driving the penalty are entirely different across doses.** How do you reconcile this with the claim of a \"constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty\" that you describe as \"systematic additive bias\"? Why does the paper present this claim when your internal analysis explicitly flagged it as a \"discretization artifact\"?\n\n3. The multi-seed dose-response (Table 3) shows the taxonomy penalty is 6.4pp at 8 of 9 seed x dose cells. Given that each cell represents a different training run with different random initialization, what is the probability of observing this level of uniformity by chance? Have you verified these numbers against the actual eval logs?\n\n4. For the pooled permutation test (N=423), how did you handle the non-independence between dose levels within the same seed? Did you use a hierarchical permutation structure?\n\n5. The \"imagined experiments\" YAML labels Multi-Seed Dose-Response and other experiments as \"imagined\" with \"expected_results\" and \"result_rationale\" explaining what the results *should* be. Were these experiments actually conducted, or are the numbers in the paper projections?\n\n### Scores\n\n**Soundness: 2 (Lacking)**\nThe Table 1 discrepancy is serious -- the paper's central table contradicts the abstract and narrative. The \"constant 6.4pp penalty\" claim contradicts the authors' own per-task analysis. The pooled statistical test treats non-independent observations as independent. Multiple experiments rely on single-seed results despite the paper arguing single-seed results are unreliable.\n\n**Excitement: 3 (Interesting)**\nThe research question is timely and practically relevant. The per-operator locality gradient is a genuinely interesting finding. The negative result, if properly supported, would be valuable for the community. The experimental breadth is impressive for a workshop paper. However, the data integrity issues severely undermine excitement.\n\n**Overall: 2.5 (Leaning Reject)**\nThe paper asks the right question and the experimental program is admirably thorough in design. However, the internal inconsistencies between Table 1 and the rest of the paper, the \"constant 6.4pp penalty\" claim that the authors' own analysis identified as a discretization artifact, and the questionable provenance of multi-seed dose-response data (labeled \"imagined\" in project files) collectively undermine confidence in the results. If the Table 1 issue is a copy-paste error and the dose-response data is real (not imagined), a revision addressing the 6.4pp artifact and the pooled test non-independence could raise this to borderline accept. The per-operator locality gradient and the variance-as-primary-finding framing are strong enough to carry the paper if the data integrity issues are resolved."}],"totalDurationMs":135205,"totalTokens":85977,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14127,"cache_read_input_tokens":68783,"output_tokens":3066,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14127},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3066,"cache_read_input_tokens":68783,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14127,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":1,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjNwRAJufHVAjSHD9zJHg7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D6Ds8qCKmHDefGoWyLRwmf","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Trivial baselines that work suspiciously well\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\n### Abstract\nTaxonomy-guided error injection—exposing LLM agents to structured failure trajectories during training—has achieved remarkable success in tool-use domains (e.g., +57pp Recovery Rate on ToolBench). They test whether this transfers to embodied planning by designing a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7% vs 65.3%) while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response reveals (1) shared threshold-plateau where both injection methods collapse 12.1±1.1pp between 10-25%, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Qualitative analysis finds \"taxonomy-operator replay.\" Results partially replicate on WebShop, persist at 50K scale. Conclusion: error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\n- **Error injection for LLM agents**: Cites PALADIN [1] (ToolScan taxonomy, 7 failure types, +57pp on ToolBench, SFT + retrieval) and EEF [2] (extracting positive from failed trajectories, WebShop). Claims their work tests injection direction in planning.\n- **Embodied planning with LLMs**: Cites ALFWorld [3], ReAct [4], SFT approaches [6]. Uses GPT-5.2 trajectories as base.\n- **Data augmentation and noise injection**: Cites Bishop 1995 [7] (noise as regularization), Natarajan 2013 [8] (noisy labels). Connects dose-response to signal-to-noise theory.\n- Total: 8 references\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- 4-operator taxonomy: P1 (step_order_swap), P2 (sub_goal_deletion), C1 (context_truncation), C4 (observation_masking)\n- 3 conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (4,358 clean + 4,671 injected, tagged), Random (same but unlabeled)\n- Dose-response: fixed 4,358 total, varying injection ratio at 10/25/50%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n- Core 3-seed: TaxEI degrades by 8.6pp, variance 2.6×\n- Dose-response: shared threshold-plateau, constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty\n- Training dynamics: TaxEI inflates clean loss 16.6%, gradient cosine 0.28\n- Qualitative: 62.5% \"wrong learning\" in 24 cases\n- WebShop: variance gradient and taxonomy penalty replicate (weaker)\n- Scale ablation (50K): deficit persists\n- Few-shot recovery: 3-shot (66.0%) > TaxEI SFT (56.7%)\n- Per-operator: locality gradient C4(+6.1) → P1(-2.7) → C1(-5.6) → P2(-8.4)\n- Statistics: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n\n### Contributions claimed\n1. First cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection\n2. Dose-response analysis decomposing injection effects\n3. Mechanistic evidence from training dynamics + qualitative analysis\n4. Error-locality gradient bridging tool-use/planning divide\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is this genuinely the first work testing error injection transfer to planning? How novel are the individual findings?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key papers missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Framing and narrative**: Is the negative result well-framed? Does it provide actionable insights?\n4. **Contribution significance for the venue**: Would Insights from Negative Results reviewers find this valuable?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Clear? Well-organized? Appropriate length?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe fair but rigorous. Evaluate against the specific venue's values.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5167,"cache_read_input_tokens":65282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5167},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"cbb69811-e2ac-4008-a3d7-e26149c4dbdd"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a66e01a30e2dfac20","tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6Ds8qCKmHDefGoWyLRwmf","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Trivial baselines that work suspiciously well\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\n### Abstract\nTaxonomy-guided error injection—exposing LLM agents to structured failure trajectories during training—has achieved remarkable success in tool-use domains (e.g., +57pp Recovery Rate on ToolBench). They test whether this transfers to embodied planning by designing a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7% vs 65.3%) while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response reveals (1) shared threshold-plateau where both injection methods collapse 12.1±1.1pp between 10-25%, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Qualitative analysis finds \"taxonomy-operator replay.\" Results partially replicate on WebShop, persist at 50K scale. Conclusion: error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\n- **Error injection for LLM agents**: Cites PALADIN [1] (ToolScan taxonomy, 7 failure types, +57pp on ToolBench, SFT + retrieval) and EEF [2] (extracting positive from failed trajectories, WebShop). Claims their work tests injection direction in planning.\n- **Embodied planning with LLMs**: Cites ALFWorld [3], ReAct [4], SFT approaches [6]. Uses GPT-5.2 trajectories as base.\n- **Data augmentation and noise injection**: Cites Bishop 1995 [7] (noise as regularization), Natarajan 2013 [8] (noisy labels). Connects dose-response to signal-to-noise theory.\n- Total: 8 references\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- 4-operator taxonomy: P1 (step_order_swap), P2 (sub_goal_deletion), C1 (context_truncation), C4 (observation_masking)\n- 3 conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (4,358 clean + 4,671 injected, tagged), Random (same but unlabeled)\n- Dose-response: fixed 4,358 total, varying injection ratio at 10/25/50%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n- Core 3-seed: TaxEI degrades by 8.6pp, variance 2.6×\n- Dose-response: shared threshold-plateau, constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty\n- Training dynamics: TaxEI inflates clean loss 16.6%, gradient cosine 0.28\n- Qualitative: 62.5% \"wrong learning\" in 24 cases\n- WebShop: variance gradient and taxonomy penalty replicate (weaker)\n- Scale ablation (50K): deficit persists\n- Few-shot recovery: 3-shot (66.0%) > TaxEI SFT (56.7%)\n- Per-operator: locality gradient C4(+6.1) → P1(-2.7) → C1(-5.6) → P2(-8.4)\n- Statistics: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n\n### Contributions claimed\n1. First cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection\n2. Dose-response analysis decomposing injection effects\n3. Mechanistic evidence from training dynamics + qualitative analysis\n4. Error-locality gradient bridging tool-use/planning divide\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is this genuinely the first work testing error injection transfer to planning? How novel are the individual findings?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key papers missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Framing and narrative**: Is the negative result well-framed? Does it provide actionable insights?\n4. **Contribution significance for the venue**: Would Insights from Negative Results reviewers find this valuable?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Clear? Well-organized? Appropriate length?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe fair but rigorous. Evaluate against the specific venue's values.","uuid":"9e1c700c-f9e5-4a55-8e8c-13c168435d1f","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a full academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue\n**Insights from Negative Results in NLP** workshop (co-located with EMNLP 2026). This workshop values:\n- Broadly applicable negative results with explanations/hypotheses\n- Ablation studies showing component contributions differ from expectations\n- Experiments on result instability (hardware, random seeds, preprocessing)\n- Trivial baselines that work suspiciously well\n- Actionable recommendations for practitioners\n- Acceptance rate: ~55-64%\n\n## Scoring Scale (ARR framework)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Mediocre, 1=Boring)\n- **Overall**: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject)\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Title**: \"Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning: Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training\"\n\n### Abstract\nTaxonomy-guided error injection—exposing LLM agents to structured failure trajectories during training—has achieved remarkable success in tool-use domains (e.g., +57pp Recovery Rate on ToolBench). They test whether this transfers to embodied planning by designing a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (unlabeled). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7% vs 65.3%) while increasing variance 2.6×. Multi-seed dose-response reveals (1) shared threshold-plateau where both injection methods collapse 12.1±1.1pp between 10-25%, and (2) constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty. Training dynamics show structured gradient interference. Qualitative analysis finds \"taxonomy-operator replay.\" Results partially replicate on WebShop, persist at 50K scale. Conclusion: error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\n- **Error injection for LLM agents**: Cites PALADIN [1] (ToolScan taxonomy, 7 failure types, +57pp on ToolBench, SFT + retrieval) and EEF [2] (extracting positive from failed trajectories, WebShop). Claims their work tests injection direction in planning.\n- **Embodied planning with LLMs**: Cites ALFWorld [3], ReAct [4], SFT approaches [6]. Uses GPT-5.2 trajectories as base.\n- **Data augmentation and noise injection**: Cites Bishop 1995 [7] (noise as regularization), Natarajan 2013 [8] (noisy labels). Connects dose-response to signal-to-noise theory.\n- Total: 8 references\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- 4-operator taxonomy: P1 (step_order_swap), P2 (sub_goal_deletion), C1 (context_truncation), C4 (observation_masking)\n- 3 conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (4,358 clean + 4,671 injected, tagged), Random (same but unlabeled)\n- Dose-response: fixed 4,358 total, varying injection ratio at 10/25/50%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n- Core 3-seed: TaxEI degrades by 8.6pp, variance 2.6×\n- Dose-response: shared threshold-plateau, constant ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty\n- Training dynamics: TaxEI inflates clean loss 16.6%, gradient cosine 0.28\n- Qualitative: 62.5% \"wrong learning\" in 24 cases\n- WebShop: variance gradient and taxonomy penalty replicate (weaker)\n- Scale ablation (50K): deficit persists\n- Few-shot recovery: 3-shot (66.0%) > TaxEI SFT (56.7%)\n- Per-operator: locality gradient C4(+6.1) → P1(-2.7) → C1(-5.6) → P2(-8.4)\n- Statistics: pooled permutation p=0.031, F-test p=0.038\n\n### Contributions claimed\n1. First cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection\n2. Dose-response analysis decomposing injection effects\n3. Mechanistic evidence from training dynamics + qualitative analysis\n4. Error-locality gradient bridging tool-use/planning divide\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is this genuinely the first work testing error injection transfer to planning? How novel are the individual findings?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key papers missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Framing and narrative**: Is the negative result well-framed? Does it provide actionable insights?\n4. **Contribution significance for the venue**: Would Insights from Negative Results reviewers find this valuable?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Clear? Well-organized? Appropriate length?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with justification)\n\nBe fair but rigorous. Evaluate against the specific venue's values."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6Ds8qCKmHDefGoWyLRwmf","session_id":"73f9f140-4f70-4f54-821d-08400ec577d5","uuid":"ac0b8ba2-3ace-4ea9-a5d1-708976e39b85","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:50:25.692Z"}
